The pointer is no longer held inside a game's window in Mission Control and App Exposé, and the hidden cursor is set again after them.

This repository's patch to the Mac driver (version 3, replacing version 2 of r8 and version 1 of r7), to be offered to
WineHQ. It needs
nothing from patches 0013 and 0014 except 0014's cursorNeedsReapply flag; without 0014 those lines are left out.

The fault: with a game in front that clips the cursor (ClipCursor; DirectInput's exclusive mouse clips to the
window), opening Mission Control kept the pointer inside the game's window area, so no other window could be
clicked; and back in the game, the Mac arrow showed over it until a Cmd-Tab out and back (mavrik S212, Skyrim SE on
macOS 27.0.1).

Why: the Mac driver's default clipping (WineConfinementClipCursorHandler, the undocumented
-[NSWindow setMouseConfinementRect:]) is kept by the window server while the app is frontmost and the window is
key. The app stays active through Mission Control and App Exposé, so the confinement stays. The older event tap handler lets events for another process through ("This may happen if the
user activates Exposé or Mission Control"), but the confinement handler has no such case. Version 1 watched for
mouse events going to other processes with a global NSEvent monitor; under the confinement macOS keeps sending the
moves to the game, so the monitor never fired (mavrik S214, hands-on test). Version 2 looked for a full-display
Dock window above the normal level; on macOS 27 the Dock's ordinary window (layer 20) covers the whole display at all
times, so version 2 read "shown" from a game's first clip and lifted it for as long as the game clipped (mavrik S215).

The fix detects the overlay itself. mavrik read this Mac's whole window list every 100 ms through each state
(S215): Mission Control and App Exposé each draw a window of WindowManager's at layer 19 covering the whole display;
WindowManager puts nothing else above the desktop level except Show Desktop's window at layer 18; nothing changes
there for a normal or a full-screen window, a full-screen Space, Spotlight, the Command-Tab switcher, a Space switch,
or an hour and three quarters of real games.
- While the app is active, the cursor is clipped and the handler is the confinement one, a timer on a background
  queue reads the window list every 100 ms (20 ms leeway), at once on activation, and at a Space change. It asks
  one question: is a window of WindowManager's (matched by the process id of com.apple.WindowManager, never by its
  name) on screen, at layer 19, covering the whole display that holds the clip rect? If a later macOS draws these
  overlays differently, it never fires, which leaves Wine's confinement as it was. It uses
  only CoreGraphics and needs no permission (window titles are withheld without Screen Recording; owner, level
  and bounds are not). It reports only a change, to the main thread.
- Overlay shown: the confinement rect is lifted (the clip rect and the Windows side's clipping state are kept),
  the next mouse move is absolute, and the cursor is marked for setting again (0014). While it is shown, a new
  ClipCursor from the game (it is deactivated and activated as Mission Control opens and clips again at once)
  keeps its rect but stays lifted, and SetCursorPos does not pull the pointer back into the game.
- Overlay gone, read twice in a row (inside Mission Control the player can move to App Exposé or another Space,
  and one read may fall between WindowManager's windows); three failed window list reads in a row count as gone, so
  the pointer is never left free for good: if the app is still active and clipping, the rect is put back, the
  pointer is moved into it as starting a clip does, and the hidden cursor is set again (at once and 250 ms later,
  as on activation). If the
  player chose another app, the app resigned and Wine's ordinary path released the pointer; the read at the next
  activation settles the state.
- The watch stops when clipping stops (unless an overlay is shown, then when it is gone) and when the app resigns.
  The event tap handler is unchanged.
- Under WINEDEBUG=+cursor (the Cocoa side has no debug channels; macdrv_main.c passes TRACE_ON(cursor) to it):
  "system overlay shown: confinement lifted", "system overlay gone: confinement restored", and once a minute, and
  when a watch ends, the read's median and worst time in microseconds. These lines lack Wine's pid:tid prefix;
  the version offered to WineHQ should send them through a real debug channel.

Checked (mavrik S215): applies to the CrossOver 26.3.0 tree after patches 0001 to 0014 with no fuzz, and 0016 to
0032 still apply after it with no fuzz; the changed files compile with the tree's warning flags with no new
warning (Xcode 26.2, x86_64). The same check, timed on an M1 Pro with macOS 27.0.1 (a command-line program that
opens no window): about 0.2 ms back to back, about 1 to 2.5 ms median at the watch's 100 ms pace (mostly waiting on
the window server; the reading thread's own CPU time about 0.05 ms). Not yet checked: Mission Control itself, which
only the player's own pointer can show (mavrik's r8 hand test).

